-- DICT-SEMANTICS §4.2 (obligation deferral, OD2) -- THE OPEN-WORLD ACCEPT ROW.
--
-- REGRESSION GUARD FOR #1939 (a declared constrained function rejected at its
-- own definition because no impl of its context is LOADED), S1, knowingly
-- landed at #1907's close-out and fixed by sprint/structured-predicate-carry.
-- #1939 had no must_fail pin, so before this row it had no guard anywhere.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER, hand-derived from §4.2, NOT captured. A goal is refutable only
-- when no instantiation of it can ever be satisfied. `match(IE, pi)` is read
-- against the LOADED module graph, and a module's graph NEVER contains its
-- dependents -- so "no impl heads at `Wrap` here" is a fact about this
-- compilation unit, not about the goal. A goal that still carries a type
-- VARIABLE therefore defers (OD2) and is adjudicated at each use site, where it
-- is ground. Neither `f1` nor `f2` is ever called, so no use site exists and
-- nothing is adjudicated: ACCEPT on all three verbs, stdout `open-world`.
--
-- 🚨 BOTH BINDINGS ARE LOAD-BEARING -- THEY TAKE DIFFERENT CHANNELS, AND THE
-- FIX HAD TO LAND ON BOTH. `f1`'s single-parameter `Tag (Wrap a)` is discharged
-- by the CALL-obligation checker (`checkOneCallObligation`, whose
-- `allConcreteHeads` gate reads `Wrap a` as concrete because it asks for a head
-- TYCON, not for groundness). `f2`'s two-parameter `Conv (Wrap a) b` is a MIXED
-- vector that leaves the body as a residual predicate and is adjudicated by
-- `residualRefutableNow` instead. MEASURED at the pre-slice base `7941e5fe`
-- (this tree, cold-built): `f2` alone rejected at the DEFINITION,
-- `No impl of Conv for Wrap a b`; with `residualRefutableNow` fixed and the call
-- channel untouched, `f1` still rejected, `No impl of Tag for Wrap a`. Either
-- binding alone grades green against half a fix.
--
-- ⚠️ PAIRED WITH s-open-world-unsatisfiable-use-site-rejected.mdk, THE ILLEGAL
-- TWIN. [W-QUIETER]: this slice turns a loud reject into an acceptance, so the
-- ACCEPT row is only half the assertion -- without the twin, deleting the
-- obligation check entirely would grade green here. The twin declares the same
-- contexts and CALLS one of them at a type nothing satisfies; it must still
-- reject, one site further out.
data Wrap a = Wrap a

interface Tag a where
  tag : a -> String

interface Conv a b where
  conv : a -> b -> String

f1 : Tag (Wrap a) => Wrap a -> String
f1 x = tag x

f2 : Conv (Wrap a) b => Wrap a -> b -> String
f2 x y = conv x y

main = println "open-world"
